## Packet Shuffling Mechanism

The `shuffle` method in ServerType0 allows for packet remapping:

```perl
sub shuffle {
    my ($self) = @_;
    
    my %shuffle;
    my $load_shuffle = Settings::addTableFile('shuffle.txt', loader => [\&FileParsers::parseDataFile2, \%shuffle], mustExist => 0);
    Settings::loadByHandle($load_shuffle);
    Settings::removeFile($load_shuffle);
    
    # Build the list of changes
    my $new = {};
    foreach (sort keys %shuffle) {
        next if !$self->{packet_list}->{$_};
        next if $self->{packet_lut}->{$handler} && $self->{packet_lut}->{$handler} ne $_;
        $new->{$shuffle{$_}} = $self->{packet_list}->{$_};
    }
    
    # Patch!
    $self->{packet_list}->{$_} = $new->{$_} foreach keys %$new;
    $self->{packet_lut}->{$new->{$_}->[0]} = $_ foreach keys %$new;
}
```

This allows OpenKore to adapt to servers that change packet IDs to prevent botting by remapping the packet IDs according to the shuffle.txt configuration file.

## Server Type Inheritance

Different server types can inherit from ServerType0 and override specific packet definitions:

```perl
package Network::Send::ServerType1;
use base qw(Network::Send::ServerType0);

sub new {
    my $class = shift;
    my $self = $class->SUPER::new(@_);
    
    my %packets = (
        # Override specific packets
        '0072' => ['map_login', 'a4 a4 a4 V C', [qw(accountID charID sessionID tick sex)]],
    );
    
    $self->{packet_list}{$_} = $packets{$_} for keys %packets;
    return $self;
}
```

This inheritance mechanism allows OpenKore to support many different server types while minimizing code duplication.

## Dynamic Packet Selection

The system dynamically selects the appropriate packet structure based on the server type and version:

```perl
$msg = $self->reconstruct({
    switch => 'master_login',
    version => $version || $self->version,
    master_version => $master_version,
    username => $username,
    password => $password,
    game_code => '0011',
    flag => 'G000',
});
```

The `reconstruct` method looks up the appropriate packet format in the packet_lut table, which may have been customized by the shuffle mechanism or server type overrides.

This flexible architecture allows OpenKore to adapt to different server implementations while maintaining a consistent internal API for the login process.